## Ruling 2: section 7 and `busy?`

**Decision: (a). Correct the sentence; `busy?` does not change.**

- **Why not (b):** a candidate load is the same fetch work as a load by hand or a module load, and it needs base-cadence steps to finish promptly. Option (b) would put a foreground/background policy into `dao.space.dht` and make head installation up to eight times slower on the idle curve (200 ms against 25 ms).
- **Cost:** the node is busy only while a candidate record is `:loading`.
  - Polling, judging, a `:loaded` candidate awaiting install, a `:failed` one waiting out its delay, and an abandoned load's residue in the client do not make it busy.
  - Under the alternating replay of 5.5 a load is always active, so the tick owner stays at 25 ms instead of backing off to 200 ms for as long as the alternation lasts.
- **`main.cljc`:** no change. `moved?` already reads `repl.dht/busy?`, so a loading candidate holds the base interval and the idle curve resumes when the load ends. Node ticks are the owner's millisecond reading, so the 5000-tick poll is late by at most the 200 ms ceiling.
- **Tests:** the four existing `busy?` assertions in `test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc` have no follower and are unaffected. Any H3 test that waits on `(not busy?)` with a follower attached must not use a candidate whose fetch never answers.

low | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md:660`; `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1461-1478` | `busy?` docstring: "holds work: binding, a load, …" | Replace "Following never makes the node `busy?`." with:

> Following alone never makes the node `busy?`: a poll, a judged trace, a loaded candidate awaiting installation, a failed one waiting out its delay and what an abandoned load leaves in the client are not work the node reports. A candidate that is `:loading` is a load like any other and makes the node `busy?` until it ends, so the tick owner holds its base interval while a head is fetched and returns to its idle curve afterwards. Under the alternating replay of 5.5 a load is always active, and the node stays at the base interval for as long as the alternation lasts.

## Ruling 3: `link.cljc` forgetting a failed record

**Decision: acceptable for H1; it is not a safety or ownership violation, and the fix belongs in H3.**

- **Reachability:** a `require` reaches a candidate's address only when a name envelope binds a name to an index manifest's address. That is malformed or hostile, never ordinary.
- **Why ownership holds:**
  - `release` re-reads the record's kind and status before removing anything, so a stale owner entry never removes a record the follower did not start.
  - `settle` re-acquires on a missing record.
  - A candidate is in no snapshot set at any point.
- **What is lost:** the retry delay is skipped, at most one extra load per user `require`. There is no amplification beyond what a `require` of any failed address already costs.
- **The real defect is older:** `dht-attempt` is kind-blind. It also forgets a user's failed index load made by hand, and waits on or tries to link records of other kinds. D3 makes that wrong.
- **Not checked:** I did not trace what `dht-link` answers for a `:loaded` candidate-kind record; I expect a refusal.

